Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string &markdown) {
    // Replace markdown tags to HTML equivalents
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex markdownRegex(R"(^(\*+\s*)(.*)$)", "<strong$1>");
    std::regex headerRegex(R"(^(\#+\s*)(.*)$)", "<h>$2</h1>");

    // Replace markdown tags
    html = std::regex_replace(html, markdownRegex, "<strong$1>");

    // Replace header tags
    html = std::regex_replace(html, headerRegex, "<h>$2</h1>");

    return html;
}
```

This function uses regular expressions to replace markdown tags with their HTML equivalents. The `markdownRegex` matches markdown stars (`*`) and replaces them with corresponding `<strong>` tags. The `headerRegex` matches header numbers (`#`) and replaces them with corresponding `<h>` tags.

Note that this function only handles basic markdown tags and does not support all markdown flavors. For more advanced parsing, you may want to use a markdown library like `libmarkdown` or `markdown-c`. Additionally, you may want to add error handling to manage invalid markdown input or unexpected tags.

